Mongoose is an ODM (Object Data Modeling) library that helps Node.js applications 
interact with MongoDB easily.
Steps to Connect MongoDB with Node.js
Step 1: Install required packages
npm init -y
npm install mongoose dotenv
 mongoose: Connects Node.js with MongoDB.
 dotenv: Manages database connection details using environment variables.
Step 2: Create a .env file
MONGO_URI=mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/college
Here, college is the database name.
Step 3: Create index.js
import mongoose from "mongoose";
import dotenv from "dotenv";
dotenv.config();
mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGO_URI)
.then(() => {
 console.log("MongoDB Connected Successfully");
})
.catch((err) => {
 console.log(err);
});
Step 4: Run the application
node index.js
Output:
MongoDB Connected Successfully
II. Accessing and Manipulating Databases
After connecting Node.js with MongoDB, we can perform different operations such 
as creating, inserting, retrieving, updating and deleting records.
These operations are commonly called CRUD operations.

Working of XMLHttpRequest
1. Create an XMLHttpRequest object.
2. Open a connection with the server.
3. Send a request to the server.
4. The server processes the request and sends a response.
5. JavaScript receives the response and updates the webpage.
Example (JavaScript)
javascript
var xhttp = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhttp.onreadystatechange = function() {
 if (this.readyState == 4 && this.status == 200) {
 document.getElementById("demo").innerHTML =
 this.responseText;
 }
};
xhttp.open("GET", "data.txt", true);
xhttp.send();
Advantages
 Reduces page loading time.
 Improves user experience.
 Reduces unnecessary server requests.
 Supports asynchronous data exchange.
